{
  "id": "80580",
  "type": "Page",
  "tags": [],
  "title": "Buffer Capacity",
  "content": {
    "bibrefs": [],
    "model": [
      {
        "children": [
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "text": "Buffer solutions do not have an unlimited capacity to keep the pH relatively constant. If we add so much base to a buffer that the weak acid is used up, no more buffering action toward the base is possible. On the other hand, if we add an excess of acid, the weak base is used up, and no more buffering action toward any additional acid is possible. In fact, we do not even need to use up all of the acid or base in a buffer to overwhelm it: its buffering action will diminish rapidly as a given component nears depletion."
              }
            ],
            "id": "jpwv3v51mgodasw",
            "type": "p"
          },
          {
            "caption": [
              {
                "children": [
                  {
                    "em": true,
                    "text": "The indicator color (methyl orange) shows that a small amount of acid added to a buffered solution of pH 8 (beaker on the left) has little effect on the buffered system (middle beaker). However, a large amount of acid exhausts the buffering capacity of the solution, and the pH changes dramatically (beaker on the right). (Credit: Modification of work by Mark Ott.) "
                  }
                ],
                "type": "p"
              }
            ],
            "children": [
              {
                "id": "17akhcwvlydyvtk",
                "text": " ",
                "type": "text"
              }
            ],
            "id": "fks0p4qlggiqrp0",
            "src": "https://d2xvti2irp4c7t.cloudfront.net/media/eb/eb8a439f31c772c8637b5b07e65c9359/CNX_Chem_14_06_exhaust.jpg",
            "type": "img",
            "width": "650"
          },
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "text": "The buffer capacity is the amount of acid or base that can be added to a given volume of a buffer solution before the pH undergoes a significant change (usually considered to be one pH unit or more). Buffer capacity depends on the amounts of the weak acid and its conjugate base that are in a buffer mixture. For example, 1 L of a 1.0-M acetic acid and 1.0-M sodium acetate solution has a greater buffer capacity than 1 L of a 0.10-M acetic acid and 0.10-M sodium acetate solution. Both solutions have the same pH, but the first solution contains 10 times more acetic acid and acetate ion, so it can neutralize much more added strong acid or base before its buffering ability is exceeded."
              }
            ],
            "id": "q9sffzx0xoeqoyi",
            "type": "p"
          },
          {
            "caption": [
              {
                "children": [
                  {
                    "em": true,
                    "text": "A buffer consisting of 0.80 M HA and 0.80 M A"
                  },
                  {
                    "em": true,
                    "sup": true,
                    "text": "−"
                  },
                  {
                    "em": true,
                    "text": " (solution on the right) has the same pH as a buffer consisting of 0.40 M HA and 0.40 M A"
                  },
                  {
                    "em": true,
                    "sup": true,
                    "text": "−"
                  },
                  {
                    "em": true,
                    "text": " (solution on the left). However, the buffer on the right has twice the concentrations of HA and A"
                  },
                  {
                    "em": true,
                    "sup": true,
                    "text": "−"
                  },
                  {
                    "em": true,
                    "text": " as the buffer on the left. Because both solutions have the same volume, the buffer on the right contains twice as many moles of HA and A"
                  },
                  {
                    "em": true,
                    "sup": true,
                    "text": "−"
                  },
                  {
                    "em": true,
                    "text": ", so it has twice the buffering capacity."
                  }
                ],
                "type": "p"
              }
            ],
            "children": [
              {
                "id": "1d6brroqd1wn9vq",
                "text": " ",
                "type": "text"
              }
            ],
            "id": "e4eypsz0vfdjft6",
            "src": "https://d2xvti2irp4c7t.cloudfront.net/media/7a/7a5f29b78c995ebb48ea8dd477cd9f0f/buffer_capacity_particulate.png",
            "type": "img",
            "width": "650"
          },
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "id": "lfx9qglhkmvncmc",
                "text": "Selection of Suitable Buffer Mixtures"
              }
            ],
            "id": "t2fyt4orv9i7ecu",
            "type": "h1"
          },
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "text": "There are three useful rules of thumb for selecting and preparing buffer solutions:"
              }
            ],
            "id": "ictzhhm6q2ma2hv",
            "type": "p"
          },
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "children": [
                  {
                    "children": [
                      {
                        "text": "A good buffer should have roughly equal concentrations its two components. In practice, to be an effective buffer, these concentrations should not differ by more than a factor of 10."
                      }
                    ],
                    "id": "2254996875",
                    "type": "p"
                  }
                ],
                "id": "pypt8mq6v3lob1m",
                "type": "li"
              },
              {
                "children": [
                  {
                    "children": [
                      {
                        "text": "To make a buffer with a pH less than 7, you should use a weak acid and the salt containing its conjugate base. To be an effective buffer, the weak acid should have a p"
                      },
                      {
                        "em": true,
                        "text": "K"
                      },
                      {
                        "sub": true,
                        "text": "a"
                      },
                      {
                        "text": " as close as possible to the pH of the buffer (within 1 pH unit)."
                      }
                    ],
                    "id": "817613125",
                    "type": "p"
                  }
                ],
                "id": "5tm1hkzi0rgo53d",
                "type": "li"
              },
              {
                "children": [
                  {
                    "children": [
                      {
                        "text": "To make a buffer with a pH greater than 7, you should use a weak base and the salt containing its conjugate acid. To be an effective buffer, the conjugate acid should have a p"
                      },
                      {
                        "em": true,
                        "text": "K"
                      },
                      {
                        "sub": true,
                        "text": "a"
                      },
                      {
                        "text": " as close as possible to the pH of the buffer (within 1 pH unit)."
                      }
                    ],
                    "id": "3344510695",
                    "type": "p"
                  }
                ],
                "id": "dqlh5sgdrmpmrku",
                "type": "li"
              }
            ],
            "id": "wyvb8vm5npwq5fx",
            "style": "decimal",
            "type": "ol"
          },
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "text": "Blood is an important example of a buffered solution, with the principal acid and ion responsible for the buffering action being carbonic acid, H"
              },
              {
                "sub": true,
                "text": "2"
              },
              {
                "text": "CO"
              },
              {
                "sub": true,
                "text": "3"
              },
              {
                "text": ", and the bicarbonate ion, HCO"
              },
              {
                "sub": true,
                "text": "3"
              },
              {
                "sup": true,
                "text": "−"
              },
              {
                "text": ". When hydronium ions enter the blood stream, they are removed primarily by the reaction: "
              }
            ],
            "id": "oxhme8g11lckr52",
            "type": "p"
          },
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "id": "ydfr9mwbt1yycd3",
                "text": " ",
                "type": "text"
              }
            ],
            "id": "olz020krb42wnb3",
            "legacyBlockRendered": "true",
            "src": "{\\mathrm H}_3\\mathrm O^+(aq)\\;+\\;\\mathrm{HCO}_3^-(aq)\\;\\;\\longrightarrow\\;\\;{\\mathrm H}_2{\\mathrm{CO}}_3(aq) \\;+\\;{\\mathrm H}_2\\mathrm O(l)",
            "subtype": "latex",
            "type": "formula"
          },
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "text": "Added hydroxide ions are removed primarily by the reaction:"
              }
            ],
            "id": "pq7b5jd1dw0ogho",
            "type": "p"
          },
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "id": "t52xktf84plhqos",
                "text": " ",
                "type": "text"
              }
            ],
            "id": "zd14hif9gqxurwf",
            "legacyBlockRendered": "true",
            "src": "\\mathrm{OH}^-(aq)\\;+\\;{\\mathrm H}_2{\\mathrm{CO}}_3(aq)\\;\\;\\longrightarrow\\;\\;\\mathrm{HCO}_3^-(aq) \\;+\\;{\\mathrm H}_2\\mathrm O(l)",
            "subtype": "latex",
            "type": "formula"
          },
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "text": "The added strong acid or base is therefore effectively converted to the much weaker acid or base of the buffer pair (H"
              },
              {
                "sub": true,
                "text": "3"
              },
              {
                "text": "O"
              },
              {
                "sup": true,
                "text": "+"
              },
              {
                "text": " is converted to H"
              },
              {
                "sub": true,
                "text": "2"
              },
              {
                "text": "CO"
              },
              {
                "sub": true,
                "text": "3"
              },
              {
                "text": " and OH"
              },
              {
                "sup": true,
                "text": "−"
              },
              {
                "text": " is converted to HCO"
              },
              {
                "sub": true,
                "text": "3"
              },
              {
                "sup": true,
                "text": "−"
              },
              {
                "text": "). To maintain homeostasis, the pH of human blood remains very near its average value of 7.4. Normal variations in blood pH are usually less than 0.1, and pH changes of 0.4 or greater are likely to be fatal."
              }
            ],
            "id": "75kq5uhte1qj9zu",
            "type": "p"
          }
        ],
        "id": "2811529906",
        "selection": {
          "anchor": {
            "offset": 0,
            "path": [
              0,
              0
            ]
          },
          "focus": {
            "offset": 0,
            "path": [
              1,
              0
            ]
          }
        },
        "type": "content"
      },
      {
        "children": [
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "children": [
                  {
                    "text": "A pH 6.50 buffer is needed for laboratory studies of red blood cells in slightly acidic solution. Determine how to make 1.00 L of this buffer with the lower concentration component having a concentration of 1.00 M."
                  }
                ],
                "id": "95gn4ppgnwx94ca",
                "type": "p"
              },
              {
                "children": [
                  {
                    "text": "Because we need to make a buffer with pH less than 7, we should use a weak acid and its conjugate base. To make a pH 6.50 buffer, we should choose a weak acid with a p"
                  },
                  {
                    "em": true,
                    "text": "K"
                  },
                  {
                    "sub": true,
                    "text": "a"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": " as close as possible to 6.50. A weak acid with a "
                  },
                  {
                    "em": true,
                    "text": "K"
                  },
                  {
                    "sub": true,
                    "text": "a"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": " between 10"
                  },
                  {
                    "sup": true,
                    "text": "−6"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": " and 10"
                  },
                  {
                    "sup": true,
                    "text": "−7"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": " will have a p"
                  },
                  {
                    "em": true,
                    "text": "K"
                  },
                  {
                    "sub": true,
                    "text": "a"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": " between 6 and 7, and we use the "
                  },
                  {
                    "children": [
                      {
                        "text": "Ionization Constants of Common Weak Acids"
                      }
                    ],
                    "href": "/course/link/iz1ln_ka_values_of_common_weak_acids_8xgif",
                    "idref": "80709",
                    "target": "new",
                    "type": "a"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": " to select an appropriate acid."
                  }
                ],
                "id": "1aoww3t24si2dir",
                "type": "p"
              },
              {
                "children": [
                  {
                    "text": "Carbonic acid (H"
                  },
                  {
                    "sub": true,
                    "text": "2"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": "CO"
                  },
                  {
                    "sub": true,
                    "text": "3"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": ", with "
                  },
                  {
                    "em": true,
                    "text": "K"
                  },
                  {
                    "sub": true,
                    "text": "a"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": " = 4.3 × 10"
                  },
                  {
                    "sup": true,
                    "text": "−7"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": ") is a good choice. We calculate its p"
                  },
                  {
                    "em": true,
                    "text": "K"
                  },
                  {
                    "sub": true,
                    "text": "a"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": ":"
                  }
                ],
                "id": "zg5syihy7teq4zc",
                "type": "p"
              },
              {
                "children": [
                  {
                    "id": "is8izkr9aw012wz",
                    "text": " ",
                    "type": "text"
                  }
                ],
                "id": "i7kpdpdqsd4gha1",
                "legacyBlockRendered": "true",
                "src": "\\mathrm pK_{\\mathrm a}\\;=\\;-\\log K_{\\mathrm a}\\;=\\;-\\log(4.3\\times10^{-7})\\;=\\;6.37",
                "subtype": "latex",
                "type": "formula"
              },
              {
                "children": [
                  {
                    "text": "The buffer will also need to contain the bicarbonate ion (HCO"
                  },
                  {
                    "sub": true,
                    "text": "3"
                  },
                  {
                    "sup": true,
                    "text": "−"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": ", the conjugate base of H"
                  },
                  {
                    "sub": true,
                    "text": "2"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": "CO"
                  },
                  {
                    "sub": true,
                    "text": "3"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": "). The source of HCO"
                  },
                  {
                    "sub": true,
                    "text": "3"
                  },
                  {
                    "sup": true,
                    "text": "−"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": " will be a salt containing it, such as sodium bicarbonate (NaHCO"
                  },
                  {
                    "sub": true,
                    "text": "3"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": ")."
                  }
                ],
                "id": "aanuy0mr6mi3vjw",
                "type": "p"
              },
              {
                "children": [
                  {
                    "text": "The Henderson-Hasselbalch equation relates the pH of the buffer, the p"
                  },
                  {
                    "em": true,
                    "text": "K"
                  },
                  {
                    "sub": true,
                    "text": "a"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": " of the weak acid, and the ratio of conjugate base to weak acid:"
                  }
                ],
                "id": "aqxgwwhn3hee715",
                "type": "p"
              },
              {
                "children": [
                  {
                    "id": "9hqw9nsrxivv246",
                    "text": " ",
                    "type": "text"
                  }
                ],
                "id": "2an79tbv0ftdfnw",
                "legacyBlockRendered": "true",
                "src": "\\mathrm{pH}\\;=\\;\\mathrm pK_{\\mathrm a}\\;+\\;\\log\\frac{\\lbrack\\mathrm{HCO}_3^-\\rbrack}{\\lbrack{\\mathrm H}_2 {\\mathrm{CO}}_3\\rbrack}",
                "subtype": "latex",
                "type": "formula"
              },
              {
                "children": [
                  {
                    "text": "We rearrange the equation to solve for the log of the ratio of conjugate base to weak acid:"
                  }
                ],
                "id": "kjfzlh2lj47e5cs",
                "type": "p"
              },
              {
                "children": [
                  {
                    "id": "q0upm347frm0fcz",
                    "text": " ",
                    "type": "text"
                  }
                ],
                "id": "hu3lzaonbu16gej",
                "legacyBlockRendered": "true",
                "src": "\\log\\frac{\\lbrack\\mathrm{HCO}_3^-\\rbrack}{\\lbrack{\\mathrm H}_2{\\mathrm{CO}}_3\\rbrack}\\; =\\;\\mathrm{pH}\\;-\\;\\mathrm pK_{\\mathrm a}\\;=\\;6.50\\;-\\;6.37\\;=\\;0.13",
                "subtype": "latex",
                "type": "formula"
              },
              {
                "children": [
                  {
                    "text": "We then take the antilog (inverse log) of both sides to solve for the conjugate base to weak acid ratio:"
                  }
                ],
                "id": "3uc3q54cmwl1e53",
                "type": "p"
              },
              {
                "children": [
                  {
                    "id": "1mun0s0jwht10wa",
                    "text": " ",
                    "type": "text"
                  }
                ],
                "id": "hy9g5jxqahlbu9c",
                "legacyBlockRendered": "true",
                "src": "\\frac{\\lbrack\\mathrm{HCO}_3^-\\rbrack}{\\lbrack{\\mathrm H}_2{\\mathrm{CO}}_3\\rbrack}\\;\\; =\\;10^{0.13}\\;=\\;1.35",
                "subtype": "latex",
                "type": "formula"
              },
              {
                "children": [
                  {
                    "text": "Any concentrations of [HCO"
                  },
                  {
                    "sub": true,
                    "text": "3"
                  },
                  {
                    "sup": true,
                    "text": "−"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": "] and [H"
                  },
                  {
                    "sub": true,
                    "text": "2"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": "CO"
                  },
                  {
                    "sub": true,
                    "text": "3"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": "] that are in this ratio will make a buffer of the desired pH, and an effective buffer will have a high buffer capacity. In this case, we are told the lower concentration component, H"
                  },
                  {
                    "sub": true,
                    "text": "2"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": "CO"
                  },
                  {
                    "sub": true,
                    "text": "3"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": ", should be 1.00 M. We then use the previously calculated ratio to determine the concentration of HCO"
                  },
                  {
                    "sub": true,
                    "text": "3"
                  },
                  {
                    "sup": true,
                    "text": "−"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": ":"
                  }
                ],
                "id": "b0d2agofsavma5x",
                "type": "p"
              },
              {
                "children": [
                  {
                    "id": "jvayi8rlts0ricm",
                    "text": " ",
                    "type": "text"
                  }
                ],
                "id": "zdyp5pdag8iklxq",
                "legacyBlockRendered": "true",
                "src": "\\frac{\\lbrack\\mathrm{HCO}_3^-\\rbrack}{\\lbrack{\\mathrm H}_2{\\mathrm{CO}}_3\\rbrack}\\; =\\;\\frac{\\lbrack\\mathrm{HCO}_3^-\\rbrack}{1.00\\;\\mathrm M}\\;=\\;1.35\\;\\;\\;\\;\\;\\Rightarrow\\;\\;\\;\\;\\; \\lbrack\\mathrm{HCO}_3^-\\rbrack\\;=\\;1.35\\;\\mathrm M",
                "subtype": "latex",
                "type": "formula"
              },
              {
                "children": [
                  {
                    "text": "We have determined that this pH 6.50 buffer should consist of 1.00 M H"
                  },
                  {
                    "sub": true,
                    "text": "2"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": "CO"
                  },
                  {
                    "sub": true,
                    "text": "3"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": " and 1.35 M HCO"
                  },
                  {
                    "sub": true,
                    "text": "3"
                  },
                  {
                    "sup": true,
                    "text": "−"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": ". To make 1.00 L of this buffer, we will need to combine 1.00 mol H"
                  },
                  {
                    "sub": true,
                    "text": "2"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": "CO"
                  },
                  {
                    "sub": true,
                    "text": "3"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": " and 1.35 mol HCO"
                  },
                  {
                    "sub": true,
                    "text": "3"
                  },
                  {
                    "sup": true,
                    "text": "−"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": " (which can be provided by 1.35 mol NaHCO"
                  },
                  {
                    "sub": true,
                    "text": "3"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": ") with enough deionized water to make 1.00 L of solution."
                  }
                ],
                "id": "fj8w0e6x2bgtcvi",
                "type": "p"
              }
            ],
            "id": "2997036823",
            "selection": {
              "anchor": {
                "offset": 0,
                "path": [
                  0,
                  0
                ]
              },
              "focus": {
                "offset": 0,
                "path": [
                  1,
